 ],
 "literal": "Forest cultivator's field's gold-like small millet's / protective offering-consuming god-to offered abundant ear of millet / without knowing having eaten a peacock, like a dancing girl / possessing frenzy with beauty suffering distress trembles / mountain of spirits lord of the land's intimacy / water-abundant with eyes becomes a thought indeed.",
 "translation": "The intimacy of the lord of the mountain of spirits, where a peacock, having unknowingly eaten the abundant ear of gold-like small millet offered to the god who consumes protective offerings, trembles in distress like a beautiful dancing girl possessed by frenzy, is now remembered by me with eyes full of tears.",
 "modern": "தினைப்புனத்தைக் காப்பவனுடைய வயலில் விளைந்த, பொன்னைப் போன்ற சிறிய தினையின், பலி ஏற்கும் கடவுளுக்குப் படைக்கப்பட்ட செழுமையான கதிரை, அறியாமல் தின்ற மயில் ஒன்று, வெறியாட்டு ஆடும் பெண்ணின் அழகோடு துன்பமுற்று நடுங்கும். அத்தகைய அச்சம் தரும் தெய்வங்கள் வாழும் மலைநாட்டினுடைய தலைவனின் நட்பு, நீர் நிறைந்த கண்களோடு என்னால் நினைக்கப்படுகின்றது.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante about her secret love affair with the hero, who is the lord of the mountain. She compares her state to that of a peacock that unknowingly ate the sacred millet offered to the deity. The peacock, having consumed the offering meant for the god, trembles in fear and distress, resembling a dancing girl possessed by a spirit during a Veriyāṭṭu ritual. Similarly, the heroine, having tasted the forbidden joy of secret love, now trembles with anxiety and fear of discovery. This Kuriñci landscape image beautifully mirrors her internal state of distress and fear of her family finding out about her love.",
